(Why normalization exists:) Tj
0 0 0 rg
/F1 10.2 Tf
1 0 0 1 66.0 653.0 Tm
(- Bad wide schemas repeat facts and create update anomalies. Example: if dept_name -> building,budget,) Tj
/F1 10.2 Tf
1 0 0 1 66.0 640.0 Tm
(  storing department facts in every faculty row means one bad update can make one department) Tj
/F1 10.2 Tf
1 0 0 1 66.0 627.0 Tm
(  appear to be in two buildings.) Tj
/F1 10.2 Tf
1 0 0 1 66.0 614.0 Tm
(- Normalization asks whether a relation is in "good" form. If not, decompose into smaller relations that) Tj
/F1 10.2 Tf
1 0 0 1 66.0 601.0 Tm
(  are good, lossless, and preferably dependency preserving.) Tj
/F1 10.2 Tf
1 0 0 1 66.0 588.0 Tm
(- Denormalization can be chosen for performance, but it trades faster reads for extra storage, update) Tj
/F1 10.2 Tf
1 0 0 1 66.0 575.0 Tm
(  work, and possible inconsistency. Materialized views are a managed denormalization pattern.) Tj
0.1 0.1 0.1 rg
/F2 10.8 Tf
1 0 0 1 54.0 554.0 Tm
(Functional dependencies:) Tj
0 0 0 rg
/F1 10.2 Tf
1 0 0 1 66.0 534.0 Tm
(- alpha -> beta means any two legal tuples that agree on all attributes in alpha must agree on all) Tj
/F1 10.2 Tf
1 0 0 1 66.0 521.0 Tm
(  attributes in beta.) Tj
/F1 10.2 Tf
1 0 0 1 66.0 508.0 Tm
(- An FD is trivial if beta is a subset of alpha.) Tj
/F1 10.2 Tf
1 0 0 1 66.0 495.0 Tm
(- A shown instance can accidentally satisfy an FD that does not hold for all legal instances. For exam) Tj
/F1 10.2 Tf
1 0 0 1 66.0 482.0 Tm
(  questions that say "based only on data shown," use only the tuple evidence.) Tj
/F1 10.2 Tf
1 0 0 1 66.0 469.0 Tm
(- Closure F+ is every FD logically implied by F. Attribute closure X+ is every attribute determined by X) Tj
/F1 10.2 Tf
1 0 0 1 66.0 456.0 Tm
(  under F.) Tj
